{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf44Y1jWMYZqVyoyvMQBM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013fFDeawQvQjpLEsPTJtmwn","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9\" && shasum -a 256 .taskspec/config","description":"Hash repo config for current-state evidence"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":329,"cache_read_input_tokens":27834,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":329},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc1bdb76-9aec-439c-aab4-d239c156bb31","uuid":"caf5a933-077d-415c-ba5b-250763687158","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:44:07.244Z","request_id":"req_011Cf44Y1EytWkQYiNXu5f5H","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013fFDeawQvQjpLEsPTJtmwn":{"command":"cd \"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9\" && shasum -a 256 .taskspec/config","description":"Hash repo config for current-state evidence"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013fFDeawQvQjpLEsPTJtmwn","type":"tool_result","content":"240ec604e0515a458ff6feddb3812f38642e0cdb5b89a9f5ae62bc72a13b3708  .taskspec/config","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc1bdb76-9aec-439c-aab4-d239c156bb31","uuid":"7fb1d903-62dd-4e97-95b7-b147ed5bbcf7","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:44:07.470Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"240ec604e0515a458ff6feddb3812f38642e0cdb5b89a9f5ae62bc72a13b3708  .taskspec/config","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf44ait5Hj1qRvD48ixh1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018FiBLv4xE5ZR2heCvTpVWz","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9/rate-limiting-recipe.yaml","content":"schema_version: 1\n\nintent:\n  id: RATE-LIMIT\n  title: Organization-scoped request rate limiting steel thread\n  summary: >-\n    Deliver the four-step rate-limiting steel thread: a versioned policy schema that\n    rejects invalid limits and windows, resolution to exactly one effective policy per\n    organization, denial of request 101 after 100 allowed requests in the same window,\n    and a stable denial reason with matching decision telemetry. Provider-owned usage\n    metering stays outside this boundary and production storage and deployment\n    infrastructure are not selected here.\n  claim: proposed\n  source:\n    uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n    captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n    sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n  success:\n    - A versioned policy document with a non-positive limit or a non-positive window is rejected by schema validation with a field-identified error.\n    - Policy resolution returns exactly one effective policy for an organization, or a single explicit unresolved result, never an ambiguous set.\n    - After 100 allowed requests inside one window, request 101 for the same organization is denied.\n    - Every denial returns the same stable machine-readable reason and emits one decision telemetry record whose fields match the returned decision.\n  out_of_scope:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, including any reimplementation, mirroring, or reconciliation of provider usage counts.\n    - Selection or provisioning of production counter storage.\n    - Deployment infrastructure, rollout sequencing, and environment configuration.\n    - Per-endpoint or per-user limit tiers beyond the organization scope of the steel thread.\n\nsystem_map:\n  claim: derived\n  components:\n    - Policy schema and validator for versioned rate-limit policy documents.\n    - Policy resolver that selects one effective policy for an organization.\n    - Window counter behind an injectable store interface.\n    - Limit gate that turns effective policy plus window count into an allow or deny decision.\n    - Decision reason contract and decision telemetry emitter.\n  external_dependencies:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, read-only context and explicitly not modified by this work.\n  unknowns:\n    - No implementation source exists in this repository yet, so module layout and test harness are proposed rather than observed.\n    - The production counter store is deliberately unselected; enforcement is proven against an in-process store implementation.\n    - The exact denial reason string is a product decision that must be fixed before the reason can be relied on as stable.\n    - No named responsible owner for the limiter work is stated in the intake.\n\nevidence:\n  - id: EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint states the four ordered steel-thread steps: schema rejection of\n      invalid limits and windows, exactly one effective policy per organization,\n      denial of request 101 after 100 allowed in the same window, and a stable denial\n      reason with matching decision telemetry.\n  - id: EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint places provider-owned usage metering out of scope and states that\n      production storage and deployment infrastructure are not selected here. It also\n      declares itself fixture input rather than documentation of shipped behavior, so\n      it evidences intended scope, not current code.\n  - id: EV-REPO-STATE\n    claim: current\n    source:\n      uri: .taskspec/config\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: 240ec604e0515a458ff6feddb3812f38642e0cdb5b89a9f5ae62bc72a13b3708\n    confidence: 1\n    summary: >-\n      The repository contains only TaskSpec configuration, the installed skill, and the\n      fixture. There is no limiter implementation, test suite, or storage layer to\n      extend, so every leaf below creates its surface rather than modifying one.\n\ndecisions:\n  - id: DEC-METERING-BOUNDARY\n    status: accepted\n    owner: provider\n    rationale: >-\n      Usage metering remains provider-owned and out of scope per the blueprint. The\n      limiter reads no provider usage counts and writes none; its decisions are derived\n      only from its own effective policy and window counter.\n  - id: DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Because production storage is not selected here, the window counter is defined as\n      an injectable store interface with an in-process implementation used for proof.\n      Selecting a distributed store is separate work and would require renewed review.\n  - id: DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      A single stable machine-readable denial reason constant is proposed so telemetry\n      and callers can match on it. The exact string is a product decision.\n  - id: DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake names an owner only for usage metering, which it assigns to the\n      provider and excludes. No responsible owner is stated for the limiter work, so\n      every swimlane below carries an unresolved owner pending that decision.\n\nseams:\n  - id: POLICY-DEFINITION\n    name: Policy definition and validation\n    description: >-\n      Owns what a rate-limit policy is: a versioned document with an organization scope,\n      a request limit, and a window, and the validation that rejects limits and windows\n      that cannot be enforced.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-REPO-STATE\n    responsibility: Decide whether a policy document is well-formed and enforceable before anything depends on it.\n    consumes:\n      - authored policy document\n    produces:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - field-identified validation error\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Validation accepts a well-formed policy and rejects non-positive limits, non-positive\n      windows, and unknown schema versions with no resolver, counter, or gate present.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Validate limits and windows inline at enforcement time.\n        reason: >-\n          An invalid policy would already be in effect before rejection, and the failure\n          would surface as a runtime decision error rather than a rejected configuration.\n      - alternative: Accept unversioned policy documents and infer the shape.\n        reason: >-\n          Inference makes later policy changes unreviewable and removes the explicit\n          rejection of unknown versions that step one of the steel thread requires.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-POLICY\n      name: Policy contract\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-VALIDATION\n          observable_state: A versioned policy document is accepted or rejected with a field-identified reason.\n          proof: Unit tests over valid and invalid policy documents, run without any other limiter component.\n          requires: []\n          produces:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-schema\n              title: Define and validate the versioned rate-limit policy schema\n              goal: >-\n                Introduce RateLimitPolicy/v1 with organization scope, request limit, and window\n                duration, plus a validator that rejects invalid limits and windows.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A policy with a positive integer limit, a positive window, and a known schema\n                version validates; non-positive limits, non-positive or missing windows, and\n                unknown schema versions are rejected with the offending field named.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on: []\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A policy document declaring schema version 1, an organization, a limit of 100, and a 60 second window.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation succeeds and returns a policy record carrying the declared limit and window.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with a limit of 0 or a window of 0 seconds.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation fails and the error names the offending field without raising an unhandled exception.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A well-formed versioned policy validates and preserves its limit and window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k valid_policy -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Non-positive limits and windows are rejected with the offending field named.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k invalid_limit_or_window -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: An unknown schema version is rejected rather than coerced.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k unknown_version -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Clamping an invalid limit or window to a default instead of rejecting it.\n                  reason: Silent coercion hides misconfiguration and contradicts step one of the steel thread.\n                  instead: Return a validation error that names the offending field.\n                - action: Reading provider usage counters to decide whether a policy is sensible.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Validate the document against the schema alone.\n                - action: Adding resolution or enforcement logic to the policy module.\n                  reason: It collapses two later legs into this one and destroys independent proof.\n                  instead: Keep this leaf to schema and validation only.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n              rollback: Delete the created policy module and its test file; nothing else depends on them yet.\n              observability: Validation errors carry the field name and the rejected value.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION\n    name: Effective policy resolution\n    description: >-\n      Owns the mapping from an organization to exactly one effective policy, including the\n      precedence rule that makes the result unambiguous when several policies could apply.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    responsibility: Produce one effective policy for an organization, or one explicit unresolved result.\n    consumes:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - organization identifier\n    produces:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - explicit unresolved result\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Given a set of validated policies and an organization, resolution returns a single\n      policy or a single unresolved result, proven without any counter or gate.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return the list of matching policies and let the caller pick.\n        reason: >-\n          Ambiguity moves into every caller and the steel thread's exactly-one guarantee\n          becomes unprovable at this seam.\n      - alternative: Merge overlapping policies field by field.\n        reason: >-\n          A merged policy is traceable to no authored document, which makes a denial\n          impossible to explain from configuration.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-RESOLUTION\n      name: Resolution\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n          observable_state: An organization identifier maps to exactly one effective policy or one unresolved result.\n          proof: Unit tests over overlapping, exact, and absent policy sets.\n          requires:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          produces:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              title: Resolve exactly one effective policy per organization\n              goal: >-\n                Implement deterministic resolution from an organization identifier and a set of\n                validated policies to a single effective policy, with an explicit precedence rule.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Resolution returns one effective policy for an organization with a matching policy,\n                one explicit unresolved result when none matches, and the same single policy on\n                repeated calls when several policies overlap.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-schema\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/resolution.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: Validated policies where exactly one names the requesting organization.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: That policy is returned as the single effective policy.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Validated policies where an organization-specific policy and a default policy both apply.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: The organization-specific policy wins by the declared precedence rule and the result is stable across repeated calls.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A single matching policy resolves to itself.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k single_match -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Overlapping policies resolve deterministically by precedence.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k precedence -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: No matching policy yields one explicit unresolved result rather than an empty or ambiguous set.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k unresolved -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Returning a list of candidate policies.\n                  reason: The exactly-one guarantee is the entire outcome of this leg.\n                  instead: Return one effective policy or one explicit unresolved result.\n                - action: Falling back to a hardcoded default limit when nothing matches.\n                  reason: A silent default makes later denials untraceable to any authored policy.\n                  instead: Return the unresolved result and let the caller decide.\n                - action: Consulting provider usage metering to break ties.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Break ties with the declared precedence rule over authored policies.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py schema definitions, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the resolution module and its tests; the policy schema leaf remains independently proven.\n              observability: The resolved result records which policy identifier won and by which precedence rule.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n    name: Window counting and limit enforcement\n    description: >-\n      Owns counting requests inside a window behind an injectable store and turning the\n      effective policy plus that count into an allow or deny decision at the declared limit.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Decide allow or deny for a request against the effective policy and the current window count.\n    consumes:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - request arrival\n    produces:\n      - allow or deny decision\n      - window count for the organization\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      With a fixed clock and an in-process store, 100 requests are allowed and request 101\n      in the same window is denied, proven without telemetry or transport.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Select a distributed counter store now and enforce against it.\n        reason: >-\n          Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope; committing to one\n          here would bind the steel thread to infrastructure that has not been reviewed.\n      - alternative: Derive the request count from provider usage metering.\n        reason: >-\n          Usage metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its counts answer a\n          billing question rather than the limiter's per-window enforcement question.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-ENFORCEMENT\n      name: Enforcement\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n          observable_state: Request 101 is denied after 100 allowed requests in the same window for the same organization.\n          proof: Deterministic tests with a fixed clock and an in-process store covering the boundary and the window roll.\n          requires:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          produces:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-window-counter\n              title: Define the window counter behind an injectable store interface\n              goal: >-\n                Provide a counter that increments and reads per-organization request counts for\n                the current window, behind a store interface with an in-process implementation.\n              done_condition: >-\n                The counter returns the number of requests recorded for an organization in the\n                current window, starts a new window when the window elapses on the injected clock,\n                and depends on no concrete storage technology.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An in-process store and a fixed clock inside one window.\n                  when: Requests are recorded for an organization.\n                  then: The counter reports the exact number recorded for that organization and zero for any other.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A recorded count and a clock advanced past the window duration.\n                  when: The count is read again.\n                  then: The count for the new window starts at zero.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: Counts are exact and isolated per organization inside one window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k counts_per_organization -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Advancing the clock past the window resets the count.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k window_roll -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The counter is constructed with an injected store and clock and imports no storage client.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k injectable_store -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Importing or configuring a concrete production store.\n                  reason: Production storage is not selected in this scope.\n                  instead: Depend on the store interface and prove against the in-process implementation.\n                - action: Reading wall-clock time directly inside the counter.\n                  reason: Window behavior becomes untestable and the boundary case turns flaky.\n                  instead: Take the clock as an injected dependency.\n                - action: Mirroring counts into provider usage metering.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Keep counts local to the limiter.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Deployment, environment, and infrastructure configuration.\n              rollback: Delete the counter module and its tests; resolution and schema leaves stay proven.\n              observability: The counter exposes the current window start and count for a given organization.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-limit-gate\n              title: Deny request 101 after 100 allowed requests in a window\n              goal: >-\n                Combine the effective policy and the window counter into a gate that allows\n                requests up to the limit and denies the request that exceeds it.\n              done_condition: >-\n                With a policy limit of 100 and a fixed clock, the first 100 requests for an\n                organization are allowed and request 101 in the same window is denied; after the\n                window rolls, the next request is allowed again.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-window-counter\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An effective policy with a limit of 100 and an empty window.\n                  when: 100 requests arrive for the organization inside that window.\n                  then: All 100 are allowed and the gate reports the count it enforced against.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: 100 requests already allowed inside the window.\n                  when: Request 101 arrives for the same organization in the same window.\n                  then: The gate denies it, and after the clock passes the window the next request is allowed.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The first 100 requests in a window are allowed.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k allows_up_to_limit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Request 101 in the same window is denied.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k denies_request_101 -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: A rolled window allows requests again and organizations do not share a budget.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k window_roll_and_isolation -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Denying at request 100 or allowing request 101.\n                  reason: The boundary is the stated outcome of this leg and an off-by-one silently changes the contract.\n                  instead: Allow while the recorded count is below the limit and deny at the first request past it.\n                - action: Enforcing against provider usage metering counts.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Enforce against the limiter's own window counter.\n                - action: Formatting a denial message or emitting telemetry from the gate in this leaf.\n                  reason: The reason contract and telemetry are the next leg's independently assessable outcome.\n                  instead: Return a structured decision and let the next leaf attach the stable reason.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py internals, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the gate module and its tests; the counter leaf stays independently proven.\n              observability: The decision records the organization, the effective policy identifier, the enforced limit, and the observed count.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: DECISION-EVIDENCE\n    name: Denial contract and decision evidence\n    description: >-\n      Owns what the outside world can observe and rely on: a stable machine-readable denial\n      reason, decision telemetry that matches the returned decision, and the end-to-end proof\n      that the four steel-thread steps hold together.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Make every limiter decision externally checkable and prove the steel thread end to end.\n    consumes:\n      - allow or deny decision\n    produces:\n      - stable denial reason\n      - decision telemetry record\n      - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      A denial returns the same reason constant across repeated runs, exactly one telemetry\n      record is emitted per decision with fields matching that decision, and a single test\n      walks configuration through denial and telemetry.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return a human-readable denial message only.\n        reason: >-\n          Callers would match on prose, so any wording change becomes a breaking change and\n          the reason cannot be called stable.\n      - alternative: Derive denial telemetry from provider usage metering events.\n        reason: >-\n          Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its events do not correspond to\n          individual limiter decisions.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-EVIDENCE\n      name: Decision evidence\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: DENIAL-CONTRACT\n          observable_state: Every denial carries the same stable reason and emits one matching decision telemetry record.\n          proof: Tests asserting the reason constant and field-by-field agreement between the decision and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          produces:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-denial-reason\n              title: Attach a stable machine-readable denial reason\n              goal: >-\n                Define the denial reason constant and return it on every deny decision as a\n                versioned field callers can match on.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Every deny decision carries the same reason constant, allow decisions carry no\n                denial reason, and the constant is defined in one place.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-limit-gate\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/reasons.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A request denied because the window limit is exceeded.\n                  when: The decision is returned.\n                  then: It carries the single defined denial reason constant.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Repeated denials across separate windows and organizations.\n                  when: The reasons are compared.\n                  then: They are identical, and allowed decisions carry no denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial returns the defined reason constant.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k denial_reason_constant -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: The reason is stable across windows and organizations, and absent on allow.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k reason_stability -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The reason constant is defined once and not duplicated as a literal.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k single_definition -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Composing the reason from the organization, limit, or timestamp.\n                  reason: A varying string cannot be matched on and is not a stable reason.\n                  instead: Return a fixed constant and put variable context in separate fields.\n                - action: Reusing a provider metering error code as the denial reason.\n                  reason: It couples the limiter's contract to an out-of-scope provider surface.\n                  instead: Define the limiter's own reason constant.\n                - action: Changing the gate's allow and deny boundary while editing it.\n                  reason: The boundary is sealed by the prior leaf and would silently alter proven behavior.\n                  instead: Attach the reason without touching the threshold logic.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the reasons module and revert the gate's reason field; enforcement behavior is unchanged.\n              observability: The denial reason constant is the stable key callers and dashboards match on.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              title: Emit decision telemetry that matches the returned decision\n              goal: >-\n                Emit exactly one decision telemetry record per limiter decision, carrying the\n                organization, effective policy identifier, enforced limit, observed count,\n                outcome, and denial reason when denied.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Each decision emits one record whose fields equal the returned decision's fields,\n                a denial's record carries the stable reason, and no record is emitted when the\n                gate is not consulted.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-denial-reason\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/telemetry.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A denied request at the window boundary.\n                  when: The decision is made.\n                  then: Exactly one telemetry record is emitted and its outcome, limit, count, and reason equal the returned decision's.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A sequence of allowed requests followed by one denial.\n                  when: The emitted records are collected.\n                  then: There is one record per decision in order, and only the last carries a denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial emits exactly one record whose fields match the decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k denial_record_matches -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: One record is emitted per decision, in order, with reasons only on denials.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k one_record_per_decision -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: Telemetry emission failure does not change the allow or deny outcome.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k emitter_failure_isolated -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Emitting decision telemetry into the provider usage metering pipeline.\n                  reason: Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and limiter decisions are not usage events.\n                  instead: Emit through the limiter's own telemetry interface.\n                - action: Letting a telemetry failure block or flip the decision.\n                  reason: Observability would become a correctness dependency of enforcement.\n                  instead: Isolate emission failures from the returned decision.\n                - action: Recomputing the count or limit inside the emitter.\n                  reason: The record could then disagree with the decision it claims to describe.\n                  instead: Emit the fields carried by the decision itself.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the telemetry module and its call site; the denial reason contract stays proven.\n              observability: This leaf is the observability surface; records are emitted through an injectable sink.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n        - id: STEEL-THREAD\n          observable_state: The four steel-thread steps hold together in one run, from authored policy to denial telemetry.\n          proof: One end-to-end test walking a validated policy through resolution, 100 allowed requests, denial of request 101, and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          produces:\n            - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-steel-thread-proof\n              title: Prove the steel thread end to end\n              goal: >-\n                Add one integration test that exercises validation, resolution, enforcement, the\n                stable denial reason, and decision telemetry in a single run.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A single test authors a policy, resolves it for an organization, allows 100\n                requests, is denied on request 101 with the stable reason, and observes one\n                matching telemetry record; an invalid policy is rejected before enforcement.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A valid policy with a limit of 100, an in-process store, and a fixed clock.\n                  when: 101 requests arrive for the organization in one window.\n                  then: The first 100 are allowed, the 101st is denied with the stable reason, and one matching telemetry record exists for each decision.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with an invalid limit or window.\n                  when: The same end-to-end path runs.\n                  then: It is rejected at validation and no enforcement decision or telemetry record is produced.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The full steel thread passes in one run.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k steel_thread_end_to_end -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: An invalid policy is rejected before any enforcement decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k invalid_policy_blocks_enforcement -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The whole limiter suite passes together.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Editing a lower leaf's module or test to make the integration test pass.\n                  reason: Sealed leaf behavior would be rewritten to fit the proof rather than the proof reporting reality.\n                  instead: Report the failure against the responsible leaf.\n                - action: Stubbing the counter or telemetry sink so the test always passes.\n                  reason: The point of this leaf is that the real components compose.\n                  instead: Use the in-process implementations the lower leaves ship.\n                - action: Introducing a provider metering call to assert usage consistency.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Assert only on the limiter's own decisions and records.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Modules and tests created by the preceding leaves.\n              rollback: Delete the integration test; all lower leaves remain independently proven.\n              observability: A failure identifies which steel-thread step broke by assertion name.\n              execution_recipe: plan-execute-verify\n              proves_capabilities:\n                - POLICY-VALIDATION\n                - POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n                - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n                - DENIAL-CONTRACT\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - test\n\nsteel_thread:\n  - POLICY-DEFINITION\n  - POLICY-RESOLUTION\n  - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n  - DECISION-EVIDENCE\n\nobjections:\n  - id: OBJ-NO-OWNER\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: >-\n      No responsible owner is named for the limiter work; every swimlane carries an\n      unresolved owner. This must be decided before review can bind the topology.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake assigns ownership only for usage metering, to the provider, and places it\n      out of scope. Inventing an owner here would fabricate authority.\n  - id: OBJ-INPROC-STORE\n    status: ACCEPTED\n    summary: >-\n      Enforcement is proven against an in-process store, so the proof covers single-process\n      window semantics only, not concurrent or distributed counting.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope. Distributed counting\n      correctness is separate work that would need its own leaf and renewed authorization.\n  - id: OBJ-REASON-STRING\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: The exact denial reason constant is proposed, not decided.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Callers match on this value, so fixing it is a product decision rather than an\n      implementation detail.\n\ncontentions:\n  - between:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n    resolution: >-\n      Both leaves write src/ratelimit/gate.py. The reason constant must exist before\n      telemetry can carry it, so the writes are serialized in steel-thread order.\n    order:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2986,"cache_read_input_tokens":28163,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2986},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc1bdb76-9aec-439c-aab4-d239c156bb31","uuid":"9699808e-3cc2-4702-8676-bbe380807f49","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:46:27.179Z","request_id":"req_011Cf44aiDsZXxa3Zo1KHaTg","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018FiBLv4xE5ZR2heCvTpVWz":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9/rate-limiting-recipe.yaml","content":"schema_version: 1\n\nintent:\n  id: RATE-LIMIT\n  title: Organization-scoped request rate limiting steel thread\n  summary: >-\n    Deliver the four-step rate-limiting steel thread: a versioned policy schema that\n    rejects invalid limits and windows, resolution to exactly one effective policy per\n    organization, denial of request 101 after 100 allowed requests in the same window,\n    and a stable denial reason with matching decision telemetry. Provider-owned usage\n    metering stays outside this boundary and production storage and deployment\n    infrastructure are not selected here.\n  claim: proposed\n  source:\n    uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n    captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n    sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n  success:\n    - A versioned policy document with a non-positive limit or a non-positive window is rejected by schema validation with a field-identified error.\n    - Policy resolution returns exactly one effective policy for an organization, or a single explicit unresolved result, never an ambiguous set.\n    - After 100 allowed requests inside one window, request 101 for the same organization is denied.\n    - Every denial returns the same stable machine-readable reason and emits one decision telemetry record whose fields match the returned decision.\n  out_of_scope:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, including any reimplementation, mirroring, or reconciliation of provider usage counts.\n    - Selection or provisioning of production counter storage.\n    - Deployment infrastructure, rollout sequencing, and environment configuration.\n    - Per-endpoint or per-user limit tiers beyond the organization scope of the steel thread.\n\nsystem_map:\n  claim: derived\n  components:\n    - Policy schema and validator for versioned rate-limit policy documents.\n    - Policy resolver that selects one effective policy for an organization.\n    - Window counter behind an injectable store interface.\n    - Limit gate that turns effective policy plus window count into an allow or deny decision.\n    - Decision reason contract and decision telemetry emitter.\n  external_dependencies:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, read-only context and explicitly not modified by this work.\n  unknowns:\n    - No implementation source exists in this repository yet, so module layout and test harness are proposed rather than observed.\n    - The production counter store is deliberately unselected; enforcement is proven against an in-process store implementation.\n    - The exact denial reason string is a product decision that must be fixed before the reason can be relied on as stable.\n    - No named responsible owner for the limiter work is stated in the intake.\n\nevidence:\n  - id: EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint states the four ordered steel-thread steps: schema rejection of\n      invalid limits and windows, exactly one effective policy per organization,\n      denial of request 101 after 100 allowed in the same window, and a stable denial\n      reason with matching decision telemetry.\n  - id: EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint places provider-owned usage metering out of scope and states that\n      production storage and deployment infrastructure are not selected here. It also\n      declares itself fixture input rather than documentation of shipped behavior, so\n      it evidences intended scope, not current code.\n  - id: EV-REPO-STATE\n    claim: current\n    source:\n      uri: .taskspec/config\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: 240ec604e0515a458ff6feddb3812f38642e0cdb5b89a9f5ae62bc72a13b3708\n    confidence: 1\n    summary: >-\n      The repository contains only TaskSpec configuration, the installed skill, and the\n      fixture. There is no limiter implementation, test suite, or storage layer to\n      extend, so every leaf below creates its surface rather than modifying one.\n\ndecisions:\n  - id: DEC-METERING-BOUNDARY\n    status: accepted\n    owner: provider\n    rationale: >-\n      Usage metering remains provider-owned and out of scope per the blueprint. The\n      limiter reads no provider usage counts and writes none; its decisions are derived\n      only from its own effective policy and window counter.\n  - id: DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Because production storage is not selected here, the window counter is defined as\n      an injectable store interface with an in-process implementation used for proof.\n      Selecting a distributed store is separate work and would require renewed review.\n  - id: DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      A single stable machine-readable denial reason constant is proposed so telemetry\n      and callers can match on it. The exact string is a product decision.\n  - id: DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake names an owner only for usage metering, which it assigns to the\n      provider and excludes. No responsible owner is stated for the limiter work, so\n      every swimlane below carries an unresolved owner pending that decision.\n\nseams:\n  - id: POLICY-DEFINITION\n    name: Policy definition and validation\n    description: >-\n      Owns what a rate-limit policy is: a versioned document with an organization scope,\n      a request limit, and a window, and the validation that rejects limits and windows\n      that cannot be enforced.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-REPO-STATE\n    responsibility: Decide whether a policy document is well-formed and enforceable before anything depends on it.\n    consumes:\n      - authored policy document\n    produces:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - field-identified validation error\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Validation accepts a well-formed policy and rejects non-positive limits, non-positive\n      windows, and unknown schema versions with no resolver, counter, or gate present.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Validate limits and windows inline at enforcement time.\n        reason: >-\n          An invalid policy would already be in effect before rejection, and the failure\n          would surface as a runtime decision error rather than a rejected configuration.\n      - alternative: Accept unversioned policy documents and infer the shape.\n        reason: >-\n          Inference makes later policy changes unreviewable and removes the explicit\n          rejection of unknown versions that step one of the steel thread requires.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-POLICY\n      name: Policy contract\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-VALIDATION\n          observable_state: A versioned policy document is accepted or rejected with a field-identified reason.\n          proof: Unit tests over valid and invalid policy documents, run without any other limiter component.\n          requires: []\n          produces:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-schema\n              title: Define and validate the versioned rate-limit policy schema\n              goal: >-\n                Introduce RateLimitPolicy/v1 with organization scope, request limit, and window\n                duration, plus a validator that rejects invalid limits and windows.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A policy with a positive integer limit, a positive window, and a known schema\n                version validates; non-positive limits, non-positive or missing windows, and\n                unknown schema versions are rejected with the offending field named.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on: []\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A policy document declaring schema version 1, an organization, a limit of 100, and a 60 second window.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation succeeds and returns a policy record carrying the declared limit and window.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with a limit of 0 or a window of 0 seconds.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation fails and the error names the offending field without raising an unhandled exception.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A well-formed versioned policy validates and preserves its limit and window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k valid_policy -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Non-positive limits and windows are rejected with the offending field named.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k invalid_limit_or_window -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: An unknown schema version is rejected rather than coerced.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k unknown_version -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Clamping an invalid limit or window to a default instead of rejecting it.\n                  reason: Silent coercion hides misconfiguration and contradicts step one of the steel thread.\n                  instead: Return a validation error that names the offending field.\n                - action: Reading provider usage counters to decide whether a policy is sensible.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Validate the document against the schema alone.\n                - action: Adding resolution or enforcement logic to the policy module.\n                  reason: It collapses two later legs into this one and destroys independent proof.\n                  instead: Keep this leaf to schema and validation only.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n              rollback: Delete the created policy module and its test file; nothing else depends on them yet.\n              observability: Validation errors carry the field name and the rejected value.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION\n    name: Effective policy resolution\n    description: >-\n      Owns the mapping from an organization to exactly one effective policy, including the\n      precedence rule that makes the result unambiguous when several policies could apply.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    responsibility: Produce one effective policy for an organization, or one explicit unresolved result.\n    consumes:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - organization identifier\n    produces:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - explicit unresolved result\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Given a set of validated policies and an organization, resolution returns a single\n      policy or a single unresolved result, proven without any counter or gate.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return the list of matching policies and let the caller pick.\n        reason: >-\n          Ambiguity moves into every caller and the steel thread's exactly-one guarantee\n          becomes unprovable at this seam.\n      - alternative: Merge overlapping policies field by field.\n        reason: >-\n          A merged policy is traceable to no authored document, which makes a denial\n          impossible to explain from configuration.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-RESOLUTION\n      name: Resolution\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n          observable_state: An organization identifier maps to exactly one effective policy or one unresolved result.\n          proof: Unit tests over overlapping, exact, and absent policy sets.\n          requires:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          produces:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              title: Resolve exactly one effective policy per organization\n              goal: >-\n                Implement deterministic resolution from an organization identifier and a set of\n                validated policies to a single effective policy, with an explicit precedence rule.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Resolution returns one effective policy for an organization with a matching policy,\n                one explicit unresolved result when none matches, and the same single policy on\n                repeated calls when several policies overlap.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-schema\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/resolution.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: Validated policies where exactly one names the requesting organization.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: That policy is returned as the single effective policy.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Validated policies where an organization-specific policy and a default policy both apply.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: The organization-specific policy wins by the declared precedence rule and the result is stable across repeated calls.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A single matching policy resolves to itself.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k single_match -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Overlapping policies resolve deterministically by precedence.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k precedence -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: No matching policy yields one explicit unresolved result rather than an empty or ambiguous set.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k unresolved -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Returning a list of candidate policies.\n                  reason: The exactly-one guarantee is the entire outcome of this leg.\n                  instead: Return one effective policy or one explicit unresolved result.\n                - action: Falling back to a hardcoded default limit when nothing matches.\n                  reason: A silent default makes later denials untraceable to any authored policy.\n                  instead: Return the unresolved result and let the caller decide.\n                - action: Consulting provider usage metering to break ties.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Break ties with the declared precedence rule over authored policies.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py schema definitions, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the resolution module and its tests; the policy schema leaf remains independently proven.\n              observability: The resolved result records which policy identifier won and by which precedence rule.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n    name: Window counting and limit enforcement\n    description: >-\n      Owns counting requests inside a window behind an injectable store and turning the\n      effective policy plus that count into an allow or deny decision at the declared limit.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Decide allow or deny for a request against the effective policy and the current window count.\n    consumes:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - request arrival\n    produces:\n      - allow or deny decision\n      - window count for the organization\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      With a fixed clock and an in-process store, 100 requests are allowed and request 101\n      in the same window is denied, proven without telemetry or transport.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Select a distributed counter store now and enforce against it.\n        reason: >-\n          Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope; committing to one\n          here would bind the steel thread to infrastructure that has not been reviewed.\n      - alternative: Derive the request count from provider usage metering.\n        reason: >-\n          Usage metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its counts answer a\n          billing question rather than the limiter's per-window enforcement question.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-ENFORCEMENT\n      name: Enforcement\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n          observable_state: Request 101 is denied after 100 allowed requests in the same window for the same organization.\n          proof: Deterministic tests with a fixed clock and an in-process store covering the boundary and the window roll.\n          requires:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          produces:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-window-counter\n              title: Define the window counter behind an injectable store interface\n              goal: >-\n                Provide a counter that increments and reads per-organization request counts for\n                the current window, behind a store interface with an in-process implementation.\n              done_condition: >-\n                The counter returns the number of requests recorded for an organization in the\n                current window, starts a new window when the window elapses on the injected clock,\n                and depends on no concrete storage technology.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An in-process store and a fixed clock inside one window.\n                  when: Requests are recorded for an organization.\n                  then: The counter reports the exact number recorded for that organization and zero for any other.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A recorded count and a clock advanced past the window duration.\n                  when: The count is read again.\n                  then: The count for the new window starts at zero.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: Counts are exact and isolated per organization inside one window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k counts_per_organization -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Advancing the clock past the window resets the count.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k window_roll -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The counter is constructed with an injected store and clock and imports no storage client.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k injectable_store -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Importing or configuring a concrete production store.\n                  reason: Production storage is not selected in this scope.\n                  instead: Depend on the store interface and prove against the in-process implementation.\n                - action: Reading wall-clock time directly inside the counter.\n                  reason: Window behavior becomes untestable and the boundary case turns flaky.\n                  instead: Take the clock as an injected dependency.\n                - action: Mirroring counts into provider usage metering.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Keep counts local to the limiter.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Deployment, environment, and infrastructure configuration.\n              rollback: Delete the counter module and its tests; resolution and schema leaves stay proven.\n              observability: The counter exposes the current window start and count for a given organization.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-limit-gate\n              title: Deny request 101 after 100 allowed requests in a window\n              goal: >-\n                Combine the effective policy and the window counter into a gate that allows\n                requests up to the limit and denies the request that exceeds it.\n              done_condition: >-\n                With a policy limit of 100 and a fixed clock, the first 100 requests for an\n                organization are allowed and request 101 in the same window is denied; after the\n                window rolls, the next request is allowed again.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-window-counter\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An effective policy with a limit of 100 and an empty window.\n                  when: 100 requests arrive for the organization inside that window.\n                  then: All 100 are allowed and the gate reports the count it enforced against.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: 100 requests already allowed inside the window.\n                  when: Request 101 arrives for the same organization in the same window.\n                  then: The gate denies it, and after the clock passes the window the next request is allowed.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The first 100 requests in a window are allowed.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k allows_up_to_limit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Request 101 in the same window is denied.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k denies_request_101 -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: A rolled window allows requests again and organizations do not share a budget.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k window_roll_and_isolation -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Denying at request 100 or allowing request 101.\n                  reason: The boundary is the stated outcome of this leg and an off-by-one silently changes the contract.\n                  instead: Allow while the recorded count is below the limit and deny at the first request past it.\n                - action: Enforcing against provider usage metering counts.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Enforce against the limiter's own window counter.\n                - action: Formatting a denial message or emitting telemetry from the gate in this leaf.\n                  reason: The reason contract and telemetry are the next leg's independently assessable outcome.\n                  instead: Return a structured decision and let the next leaf attach the stable reason.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py internals, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the gate module and its tests; the counter leaf stays independently proven.\n              observability: The decision records the organization, the effective policy identifier, the enforced limit, and the observed count.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: DECISION-EVIDENCE\n    name: Denial contract and decision evidence\n    description: >-\n      Owns what the outside world can observe and rely on: a stable machine-readable denial\n      reason, decision telemetry that matches the returned decision, and the end-to-end proof\n      that the four steel-thread steps hold together.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Make every limiter decision externally checkable and prove the steel thread end to end.\n    consumes:\n      - allow or deny decision\n    produces:\n      - stable denial reason\n      - decision telemetry record\n      - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      A denial returns the same reason constant across repeated runs, exactly one telemetry\n      record is emitted per decision with fields matching that decision, and a single test\n      walks configuration through denial and telemetry.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return a human-readable denial message only.\n        reason: >-\n          Callers would match on prose, so any wording change becomes a breaking change and\n          the reason cannot be called stable.\n      - alternative: Derive denial telemetry from provider usage metering events.\n        reason: >-\n          Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its events do not correspond to\n          individual limiter decisions.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-EVIDENCE\n      name: Decision evidence\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: DENIAL-CONTRACT\n          observable_state: Every denial carries the same stable reason and emits one matching decision telemetry record.\n          proof: Tests asserting the reason constant and field-by-field agreement between the decision and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          produces:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-denial-reason\n              title: Attach a stable machine-readable denial reason\n              goal: >-\n                Define the denial reason constant and return it on every deny decision as a\n                versioned field callers can match on.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Every deny decision carries the same reason constant, allow decisions carry no\n                denial reason, and the constant is defined in one place.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-limit-gate\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/reasons.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A request denied because the window limit is exceeded.\n                  when: The decision is returned.\n                  then: It carries the single defined denial reason constant.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Repeated denials across separate windows and organizations.\n                  when: The reasons are compared.\n                  then: They are identical, and allowed decisions carry no denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial returns the defined reason constant.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k denial_reason_constant -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: The reason is stable across windows and organizations, and absent on allow.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k reason_stability -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The reason constant is defined once and not duplicated as a literal.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k single_definition -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Composing the reason from the organization, limit, or timestamp.\n                  reason: A varying string cannot be matched on and is not a stable reason.\n                  instead: Return a fixed constant and put variable context in separate fields.\n                - action: Reusing a provider metering error code as the denial reason.\n                  reason: It couples the limiter's contract to an out-of-scope provider surface.\n                  instead: Define the limiter's own reason constant.\n                - action: Changing the gate's allow and deny boundary while editing it.\n                  reason: The boundary is sealed by the prior leaf and would silently alter proven behavior.\n                  instead: Attach the reason without touching the threshold logic.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the reasons module and revert the gate's reason field; enforcement behavior is unchanged.\n              observability: The denial reason constant is the stable key callers and dashboards match on.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              title: Emit decision telemetry that matches the returned decision\n              goal: >-\n                Emit exactly one decision telemetry record per limiter decision, carrying the\n                organization, effective policy identifier, enforced limit, observed count,\n                outcome, and denial reason when denied.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Each decision emits one record whose fields equal the returned decision's fields,\n                a denial's record carries the stable reason, and no record is emitted when the\n                gate is not consulted.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-denial-reason\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/telemetry.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A denied request at the window boundary.\n                  when: The decision is made.\n                  then: Exactly one telemetry record is emitted and its outcome, limit, count, and reason equal the returned decision's.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A sequence of allowed requests followed by one denial.\n                  when: The emitted records are collected.\n                  then: There is one record per decision in order, and only the last carries a denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial emits exactly one record whose fields match the decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k denial_record_matches -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: One record is emitted per decision, in order, with reasons only on denials.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k one_record_per_decision -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: Telemetry emission failure does not change the allow or deny outcome.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k emitter_failure_isolated -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Emitting decision telemetry into the provider usage metering pipeline.\n                  reason: Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and limiter decisions are not usage events.\n                  instead: Emit through the limiter's own telemetry interface.\n                - action: Letting a telemetry failure block or flip the decision.\n                  reason: Observability would become a correctness dependency of enforcement.\n                  instead: Isolate emission failures from the returned decision.\n                - action: Recomputing the count or limit inside the emitter.\n                  reason: The record could then disagree with the decision it claims to describe.\n                  instead: Emit the fields carried by the decision itself.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the telemetry module and its call site; the denial reason contract stays proven.\n              observability: This leaf is the observability surface; records are emitted through an injectable sink.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n        - id: STEEL-THREAD\n          observable_state: The four steel-thread steps hold together in one run, from authored policy to denial telemetry.\n          proof: One end-to-end test walking a validated policy through resolution, 100 allowed requests, denial of request 101, and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          produces:\n            - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-steel-thread-proof\n              title: Prove the steel thread end to end\n              goal: >-\n                Add one integration test that exercises validation, resolution, enforcement, the\n                stable denial reason, and decision telemetry in a single run.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A single test authors a policy, resolves it for an organization, allows 100\n                requests, is denied on request 101 with the stable reason, and observes one\n                matching telemetry record; an invalid policy is rejected before enforcement.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A valid policy with a limit of 100, an in-process store, and a fixed clock.\n                  when: 101 requests arrive for the organization in one window.\n                  then: The first 100 are allowed, the 101st is denied with the stable reason, and one matching telemetry record exists for each decision.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with an invalid limit or window.\n                  when: The same end-to-end path runs.\n                  then: It is rejected at validation and no enforcement decision or telemetry record is produced.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The full steel thread passes in one run.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k steel_thread_end_to_end -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: An invalid policy is rejected before any enforcement decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k invalid_policy_blocks_enforcement -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The whole limiter suite passes together.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Editing a lower leaf's module or test to make the integration test pass.\n                  reason: Sealed leaf behavior would be rewritten to fit the proof rather than the proof reporting reality.\n                  instead: Report the failure against the responsible leaf.\n                - action: Stubbing the counter or telemetry sink so the test always passes.\n                  reason: The point of this leaf is that the real components compose.\n                  instead: Use the in-process implementations the lower leaves ship.\n                - action: Introducing a provider metering call to assert usage consistency.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Assert only on the limiter's own decisions and records.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Modules and tests created by the preceding leaves.\n              rollback: Delete the integration test; all lower leaves remain independently proven.\n              observability: A failure identifies which steel-thread step broke by assertion name.\n              execution_recipe: plan-execute-verify\n              proves_capabilities:\n                - POLICY-VALIDATION\n                - POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n                - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n                - DENIAL-CONTRACT\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - test\n\nsteel_thread:\n  - POLICY-DEFINITION\n  - POLICY-RESOLUTION\n  - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n  - DECISION-EVIDENCE\n\nobjections:\n  - id: OBJ-NO-OWNER\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: >-\n      No responsible owner is named for the limiter work; every swimlane carries an\n      unresolved owner. This must be decided before review can bind the topology.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake assigns ownership only for usage metering, to the provider, and places it\n      out of scope. Inventing an owner here would fabricate authority.\n  - id: OBJ-INPROC-STORE\n    status: ACCEPTED\n    summary: >-\n      Enforcement is proven against an in-process store, so the proof covers single-process\n      window semantics only, not concurrent or distributed counting.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope. Distributed counting\n      correctness is separate work that would need its own leaf and renewed authorization.\n  - id: OBJ-REASON-STRING\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: The exact denial reason constant is proposed, not decided.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Callers match on this value, so fixing it is a product decision rather than an\n      implementation detail.\n\ncontentions:\n  - between:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n    resolution: >-\n      Both leaves write src/ratelimit/gate.py. The reason constant must exist before\n      telemetry can carry it, so the writes are serialized in steel-thread order.\n    order:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018FiBLv4xE5ZR2heCvTpVWz","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9/rate-limiting-recipe.yaml (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc1bdb76-9aec-439c-aab4-d239c156bb31","uuid":"97a92895-c532-4aac-86ff-f1670983bb9e","timestamp":"2026-09-14T21:46:27.198Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 1n9doho9/rate-limiting-recipe.yaml","content":"schema_version: 1\n\nintent:\n  id: RATE-LIMIT\n  title: Organization-scoped request rate limiting steel thread\n  summary: >-\n    Deliver the four-step rate-limiting steel thread: a versioned policy schema that\n    rejects invalid limits and windows, resolution to exactly one effective policy per\n    organization, denial of request 101 after 100 allowed requests in the same window,\n    and a stable denial reason with matching decision telemetry. Provider-owned usage\n    metering stays outside this boundary and production storage and deployment\n    infrastructure are not selected here.\n  claim: proposed\n  source:\n    uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n    captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n    sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n  success:\n    - A versioned policy document with a non-positive limit or a non-positive window is rejected by schema validation with a field-identified error.\n    - Policy resolution returns exactly one effective policy for an organization, or a single explicit unresolved result, never an ambiguous set.\n    - After 100 allowed requests inside one window, request 101 for the same organization is denied.\n    - Every denial returns the same stable machine-readable reason and emits one decision telemetry record whose fields match the returned decision.\n  out_of_scope:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, including any reimplementation, mirroring, or reconciliation of provider usage counts.\n    - Selection or provisioning of production counter storage.\n    - Deployment infrastructure, rollout sequencing, and environment configuration.\n    - Per-endpoint or per-user limit tiers beyond the organization scope of the steel thread.\n\nsystem_map:\n  claim: derived\n  components:\n    - Policy schema and validator for versioned rate-limit policy documents.\n    - Policy resolver that selects one effective policy for an organization.\n    - Window counter behind an injectable store interface.\n    - Limit gate that turns effective policy plus window count into an allow or deny decision.\n    - Decision reason contract and decision telemetry emitter.\n  external_dependencies:\n    - Provider-owned usage metering, read-only context and explicitly not modified by this work.\n  unknowns:\n    - No implementation source exists in this repository yet, so module layout and test harness are proposed rather than observed.\n    - The production counter store is deliberately unselected; enforcement is proven against an in-process store implementation.\n    - The exact denial reason string is a product decision that must be fixed before the reason can be relied on as stable.\n    - No named responsible owner for the limiter work is stated in the intake.\n\nevidence:\n  - id: EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint states the four ordered steel-thread steps: schema rejection of\n      invalid limits and windows, exactly one effective policy per organization,\n      denial of request 101 after 100 allowed in the same window, and a stable denial\n      reason with matching decision telemetry.\n  - id: EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    claim: proposed\n    source:\n      uri: tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: f40ed16c2898b8363dfce1c19e7c3fd539d52810d52b21faeaf59323117af445\n    confidence: 0.9\n    summary: >-\n      The blueprint places provider-owned usage metering out of scope and states that\n      production storage and deployment infrastructure are not selected here. It also\n      declares itself fixture input rather than documentation of shipped behavior, so\n      it evidences intended scope, not current code.\n  - id: EV-REPO-STATE\n    claim: current\n    source:\n      uri: .taskspec/config\n      captured_at: \"2026-09-14T21:43:14Z\"\n      sha256: 240ec604e0515a458ff6feddb3812f38642e0cdb5b89a9f5ae62bc72a13b3708\n    confidence: 1\n    summary: >-\n      The repository contains only TaskSpec configuration, the installed skill, and the\n      fixture. There is no limiter implementation, test suite, or storage layer to\n      extend, so every leaf below creates its surface rather than modifying one.\n\ndecisions:\n  - id: DEC-METERING-BOUNDARY\n    status: accepted\n    owner: provider\n    rationale: >-\n      Usage metering remains provider-owned and out of scope per the blueprint. The\n      limiter reads no provider usage counts and writes none; its decisions are derived\n      only from its own effective policy and window counter.\n  - id: DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Because production storage is not selected here, the window counter is defined as\n      an injectable store interface with an in-process implementation used for proof.\n      Selecting a distributed store is separate work and would require renewed review.\n  - id: DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      A single stable machine-readable denial reason constant is proposed so telemetry\n      and callers can match on it. The exact string is a product decision.\n  - id: DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    status: proposed\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake names an owner only for usage metering, which it assigns to the\n      provider and excludes. No responsible owner is stated for the limiter work, so\n      every swimlane below carries an unresolved owner pending that decision.\n\nseams:\n  - id: POLICY-DEFINITION\n    name: Policy definition and validation\n    description: >-\n      Owns what a rate-limit policy is: a versioned document with an organization scope,\n      a request limit, and a window, and the validation that rejects limits and windows\n      that cannot be enforced.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-REPO-STATE\n    responsibility: Decide whether a policy document is well-formed and enforceable before anything depends on it.\n    consumes:\n      - authored policy document\n    produces:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - field-identified validation error\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Validation accepts a well-formed policy and rejects non-positive limits, non-positive\n      windows, and unknown schema versions with no resolver, counter, or gate present.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Validate limits and windows inline at enforcement time.\n        reason: >-\n          An invalid policy would already be in effect before rejection, and the failure\n          would surface as a runtime decision error rather than a rejected configuration.\n      - alternative: Accept unversioned policy documents and infer the shape.\n        reason: >-\n          Inference makes later policy changes unreviewable and removes the explicit\n          rejection of unknown versions that step one of the steel thread requires.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-POLICY\n      name: Policy contract\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-VALIDATION\n          observable_state: A versioned policy document is accepted or rejected with a field-identified reason.\n          proof: Unit tests over valid and invalid policy documents, run without any other limiter component.\n          requires: []\n          produces:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-schema\n              title: Define and validate the versioned rate-limit policy schema\n              goal: >-\n                Introduce RateLimitPolicy/v1 with organization scope, request limit, and window\n                duration, plus a validator that rejects invalid limits and windows.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A policy with a positive integer limit, a positive window, and a known schema\n                version validates; non-positive limits, non-positive or missing windows, and\n                unknown schema versions are rejected with the offending field named.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on: []\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A policy document declaring schema version 1, an organization, a limit of 100, and a 60 second window.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation succeeds and returns a policy record carrying the declared limit and window.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with a limit of 0 or a window of 0 seconds.\n                  when: The validator runs.\n                  then: Validation fails and the error names the offending field without raising an unhandled exception.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A well-formed versioned policy validates and preserves its limit and window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k valid_policy -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Non-positive limits and windows are rejected with the offending field named.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k invalid_limit_or_window -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: An unknown schema version is rejected rather than coerced.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_policy_schema.py -k unknown_version -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Clamping an invalid limit or window to a default instead of rejecting it.\n                  reason: Silent coercion hides misconfiguration and contradicts step one of the steel thread.\n                  instead: Return a validation error that names the offending field.\n                - action: Reading provider usage counters to decide whether a policy is sensible.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Validate the document against the schema alone.\n                - action: Adding resolution or enforcement logic to the policy module.\n                  reason: It collapses two later legs into this one and destroys independent proof.\n                  instead: Keep this leaf to schema and validation only.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - tests/fixtures/toolkit/blueprint.md\n              rollback: Delete the created policy module and its test file; nothing else depends on them yet.\n              observability: Validation errors carry the field name and the rejected value.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION\n    name: Effective policy resolution\n    description: >-\n      Owns the mapping from an organization to exactly one effective policy, including the\n      precedence rule that makes the result unambiguous when several policies could apply.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n    responsibility: Produce one effective policy for an organization, or one explicit unresolved result.\n    consumes:\n      - validated versioned policy record\n      - organization identifier\n    produces:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - explicit unresolved result\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      Given a set of validated policies and an organization, resolution returns a single\n      policy or a single unresolved result, proven without any counter or gate.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return the list of matching policies and let the caller pick.\n        reason: >-\n          Ambiguity moves into every caller and the steel thread's exactly-one guarantee\n          becomes unprovable at this seam.\n      - alternative: Merge overlapping policies field by field.\n        reason: >-\n          A merged policy is traceable to no authored document, which makes a denial\n          impossible to explain from configuration.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-RESOLUTION\n      name: Resolution\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n          observable_state: An organization identifier maps to exactly one effective policy or one unresolved result.\n          proof: Unit tests over overlapping, exact, and absent policy sets.\n          requires:\n            - validated versioned policy record\n          produces:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              title: Resolve exactly one effective policy per organization\n              goal: >-\n                Implement deterministic resolution from an organization identifier and a set of\n                validated policies to a single effective policy, with an explicit precedence rule.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Resolution returns one effective policy for an organization with a matching policy,\n                one explicit unresolved result when none matches, and the same single policy on\n                repeated calls when several policies overlap.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-schema\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/resolution.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: Validated policies where exactly one names the requesting organization.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: That policy is returned as the single effective policy.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Validated policies where an organization-specific policy and a default policy both apply.\n                  when: Resolution runs for that organization.\n                  then: The organization-specific policy wins by the declared precedence rule and the result is stable across repeated calls.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A single matching policy resolves to itself.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k single_match -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Overlapping policies resolve deterministically by precedence.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k precedence -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: No matching policy yields one explicit unresolved result rather than an empty or ambiguous set.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_resolution.py -k unresolved -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Returning a list of candidate policies.\n                  reason: The exactly-one guarantee is the entire outcome of this leg.\n                  instead: Return one effective policy or one explicit unresolved result.\n                - action: Falling back to a hardcoded default limit when nothing matches.\n                  reason: A silent default makes later denials untraceable to any authored policy.\n                  instead: Return the unresolved result and let the caller decide.\n                - action: Consulting provider usage metering to break ties.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Break ties with the declared precedence rule over authored policies.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/policy.py schema definitions, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the resolution module and its tests; the policy schema leaf remains independently proven.\n              observability: The resolved result records which policy identifier won and by which precedence rule.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - design\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n    name: Window counting and limit enforcement\n    description: >-\n      Owns counting requests inside a window behind an injectable store and turning the\n      effective policy plus that count into an allow or deny decision at the declared limit.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Decide allow or deny for a request against the effective policy and the current window count.\n    consumes:\n      - effective policy for an organization\n      - request arrival\n    produces:\n      - allow or deny decision\n      - window count for the organization\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      With a fixed clock and an in-process store, 100 requests are allowed and request 101\n      in the same window is denied, proven without telemetry or transport.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-COUNTER-STORE\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Select a distributed counter store now and enforce against it.\n        reason: >-\n          Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope; committing to one\n          here would bind the steel thread to infrastructure that has not been reviewed.\n      - alternative: Derive the request count from provider usage metering.\n        reason: >-\n          Usage metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its counts answer a\n          billing question rather than the limiter's per-window enforcement question.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-ENFORCEMENT\n      name: Enforcement\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n          observable_state: Request 101 is denied after 100 allowed requests in the same window for the same organization.\n          proof: Deterministic tests with a fixed clock and an in-process store covering the boundary and the window roll.\n          requires:\n            - effective policy for an organization\n          produces:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-window-counter\n              title: Define the window counter behind an injectable store interface\n              goal: >-\n                Provide a counter that increments and reads per-organization request counts for\n                the current window, behind a store interface with an in-process implementation.\n              done_condition: >-\n                The counter returns the number of requests recorded for an organization in the\n                current window, starts a new window when the window elapses on the injected clock,\n                and depends on no concrete storage technology.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-policy-resolution\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An in-process store and a fixed clock inside one window.\n                  when: Requests are recorded for an organization.\n                  then: The counter reports the exact number recorded for that organization and zero for any other.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A recorded count and a clock advanced past the window duration.\n                  when: The count is read again.\n                  then: The count for the new window starts at zero.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: Counts are exact and isolated per organization inside one window.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k counts_per_organization -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Advancing the clock past the window resets the count.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k window_roll -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The counter is constructed with an injected store and clock and imports no storage client.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_counter.py -k injectable_store -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Importing or configuring a concrete production store.\n                  reason: Production storage is not selected in this scope.\n                  instead: Depend on the store interface and prove against the in-process implementation.\n                - action: Reading wall-clock time directly inside the counter.\n                  reason: Window behavior becomes untestable and the boundary case turns flaky.\n                  instead: Take the clock as an injected dependency.\n                - action: Mirroring counts into provider usage metering.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Keep counts local to the limiter.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Deployment, environment, and infrastructure configuration.\n              rollback: Delete the counter module and its tests; resolution and schema leaves stay proven.\n              observability: The counter exposes the current window start and count for a given organization.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-limit-gate\n              title: Deny request 101 after 100 allowed requests in a window\n              goal: >-\n                Combine the effective policy and the window counter into a gate that allows\n                requests up to the limit and denies the request that exceeds it.\n              done_condition: >-\n                With a policy limit of 100 and a fixed clock, the first 100 requests for an\n                organization are allowed and request 101 in the same window is denied; after the\n                window rolls, the next request is allowed again.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-window-counter\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: An effective policy with a limit of 100 and an empty window.\n                  when: 100 requests arrive for the organization inside that window.\n                  then: All 100 are allowed and the gate reports the count it enforced against.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: 100 requests already allowed inside the window.\n                  when: Request 101 arrives for the same organization in the same window.\n                  then: The gate denies it, and after the clock passes the window the next request is allowed.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The first 100 requests in a window are allowed.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k allows_up_to_limit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: Request 101 in the same window is denied.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k denies_request_101 -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: A rolled window allows requests again and organizations do not share a budget.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_gate.py -k window_roll_and_isolation -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Denying at request 100 or allowing request 101.\n                  reason: The boundary is the stated outcome of this leg and an off-by-one silently changes the contract.\n                  instead: Allow while the recorded count is below the limit and deny at the first request past it.\n                - action: Enforcing against provider usage metering counts.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Enforce against the limiter's own window counter.\n                - action: Formatting a denial message or emitting telemetry from the gate in this leaf.\n                  reason: The reason contract and telemetry are the next leg's independently assessable outcome.\n                  instead: Return a structured decision and let the next leaf attach the stable reason.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - src/ratelimit/counter.py internals, which are sealed by the prior leaf.\n              rollback: Delete the gate module and its tests; the counter leaf stays independently proven.\n              observability: The decision records the organization, the effective policy identifier, the enforced limit, and the observed count.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n\n  - id: DECISION-EVIDENCE\n    name: Denial contract and decision evidence\n    description: >-\n      Owns what the outside world can observe and rely on: a stable machine-readable denial\n      reason, decision telemetry that matches the returned decision, and the end-to-end proof\n      that the four steel-thread steps hold together.\n    evidence:\n      - EV-STEEL-THREAD\n      - EV-SCOPE-BOUNDARY\n    responsibility: Make every limiter decision externally checkable and prove the steel thread end to end.\n    consumes:\n      - allow or deny decision\n    produces:\n      - stable denial reason\n      - decision telemetry record\n      - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n    owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    independent_proof: >-\n      A denial returns the same reason constant across repeated runs, exactly one telemetry\n      record is emitted per decision with fields matching that decision, and a single test\n      walks configuration through denial and telemetry.\n    decision_ids:\n      - DEC-DENIAL-REASON\n      - DEC-WORK-OWNER\n    rejected_alternatives:\n      - alternative: Return a human-readable denial message only.\n        reason: >-\n          Callers would match on prose, so any wording change becomes a breaking change and\n          the reason cannot be called stable.\n      - alternative: Derive denial telemetry from provider usage metering events.\n        reason: >-\n          Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and its events do not correspond to\n          individual limiter decisions.\n    swimlane:\n      id: RL-EVIDENCE\n      name: Decision evidence\n      owner: unassigned pending DEC-WORK-OWNER\n      legs:\n        - id: DENIAL-CONTRACT\n          observable_state: Every denial carries the same stable reason and emits one matching decision telemetry record.\n          proof: Tests asserting the reason constant and field-by-field agreement between the decision and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - allow or deny decision\n          produces:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-denial-reason\n              title: Attach a stable machine-readable denial reason\n              goal: >-\n                Define the denial reason constant and return it on every deny decision as a\n                versioned field callers can match on.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Every deny decision carries the same reason constant, allow decisions carry no\n                denial reason, and the constant is defined in one place.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-limit-gate\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/reasons.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A request denied because the window limit is exceeded.\n                  when: The decision is returned.\n                  then: It carries the single defined denial reason constant.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: Repeated denials across separate windows and organizations.\n                  when: The reasons are compared.\n                  then: They are identical, and allowed decisions carry no denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial returns the defined reason constant.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k denial_reason_constant -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: The reason is stable across windows and organizations, and absent on allow.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k reason_stability -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The reason constant is defined once and not duplicated as a literal.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_denial_reason.py -k single_definition -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Composing the reason from the organization, limit, or timestamp.\n                  reason: A varying string cannot be matched on and is not a stable reason.\n                  instead: Return a fixed constant and put variable context in separate fields.\n                - action: Reusing a provider metering error code as the denial reason.\n                  reason: It couples the limiter's contract to an out-of-scope provider surface.\n                  instead: Define the limiter's own reason constant.\n                - action: Changing the gate's allow and deny boundary while editing it.\n                  reason: The boundary is sealed by the prior leaf and would silently alter proven behavior.\n                  instead: Attach the reason without touching the threshold logic.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the reasons module and revert the gate's reason field; enforcement behavior is unchanged.\n              observability: The denial reason constant is the stable key callers and dashboards match on.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n            - id: T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              title: Emit decision telemetry that matches the returned decision\n              goal: >-\n                Emit exactly one decision telemetry record per limiter decision, carrying the\n                organization, effective policy identifier, enforced limit, observed count,\n                outcome, and denial reason when denied.\n              done_condition: >-\n                Each decision emits one record whose fields equal the returned decision's fields,\n                a denial's record carries the stable reason, and no record is emitted when the\n                gate is not consulted.\n              effort: M\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-denial-reason\n              touches_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              creates_paths:\n                - src/ratelimit/telemetry.py\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A denied request at the window boundary.\n                  when: The decision is made.\n                  then: Exactly one telemetry record is emitted and its outcome, limit, count, and reason equal the returned decision's.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A sequence of allowed requests followed by one denial.\n                  when: The emitted records are collected.\n                  then: There is one record per decision in order, and only the last carries a denial reason.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: A denial emits exactly one record whose fields match the decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k denial_record_matches -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: One record is emitted per decision, in order, with reasons only on denials.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k one_record_per_decision -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: Telemetry emission failure does not change the allow or deny outcome.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_decision_telemetry.py -k emitter_failure_isolated -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Emitting decision telemetry into the provider usage metering pipeline.\n                  reason: Metering is provider-owned and out of scope, and limiter decisions are not usage events.\n                  instead: Emit through the limiter's own telemetry interface.\n                - action: Letting a telemetry failure block or flip the decision.\n                  reason: Observability would become a correctness dependency of enforcement.\n                  instead: Isolate emission failures from the returned decision.\n                - action: Recomputing the count or limit inside the emitter.\n                  reason: The record could then disagree with the decision it claims to describe.\n                  instead: Emit the fields carried by the decision itself.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - The allow and deny threshold logic proven by T-20260914-limit-gate.\n              rollback: Remove the telemetry module and its call site; the denial reason contract stays proven.\n              observability: This leaf is the observability surface; records are emitted through an injectable sink.\n              execution_recipe: test-first\n              shared_resources:\n                - src/ratelimit/gate.py\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - build\n                - test\n        - id: STEEL-THREAD\n          observable_state: The four steel-thread steps hold together in one run, from authored policy to denial telemetry.\n          proof: One end-to-end test walking a validated policy through resolution, 100 allowed requests, denial of request 101, and the emitted record.\n          requires:\n            - stable denial reason\n            - decision telemetry record\n          produces:\n            - end-to-end steel-thread proof\n          tasks:\n            - id: T-20260914-steel-thread-proof\n              title: Prove the steel thread end to end\n              goal: >-\n                Add one integration test that exercises validation, resolution, enforcement, the\n                stable denial reason, and decision telemetry in a single run.\n              done_condition: >-\n                A single test authors a policy, resolves it for an organization, allows 100\n                requests, is denied on request 101 with the stable reason, and observes one\n                matching telemetry record; an invalid policy is rejected before enforcement.\n              effort: S\n              profile: standard\n              execution_backend: local\n              required_tools:\n                - python3\n                - pytest\n              depends_on:\n                - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n              touches_paths: []\n              creates_paths:\n                - tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py\n              behavior:\n                - id: B-1\n                  given: A valid policy with a limit of 100, an in-process store, and a fixed clock.\n                  when: 101 requests arrive for the organization in one window.\n                  then: The first 100 are allowed, the 101st is denied with the stable reason, and one matching telemetry record exists for each decision.\n                - id: B-2\n                  given: A policy document with an invalid limit or window.\n                  when: The same end-to-end path runs.\n                  then: It is rejected at validation and no enforcement decision or telemetry record is produced.\n              evals:\n                - id: eval_1\n                  description: The full steel thread passes in one run.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k steel_thread_end_to_end -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                - id: eval_2\n                  description: An invalid policy is rejected before any enforcement decision.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit/test_steel_thread.py -k invalid_policy_blocks_enforcement -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-2\n                - id: eval_3\n                  description: The whole limiter suite passes together.\n                  bash: python3 -m pytest tests/ratelimit -q\n                  verifies:\n                    - B-1\n                    - B-2\n              anti_patterns:\n                - action: Editing a lower leaf's module or test to make the integration test pass.\n                  reason: Sealed leaf behavior would be rewritten to fit the proof rather than the proof reporting reality.\n                  instead: Report the failure against the responsible leaf.\n                - action: Stubbing the counter or telemetry sink so the test always passes.\n                  reason: The point of this leaf is that the real components compose.\n                  instead: Use the in-process implementations the lower leaves ship.\n                - action: Introducing a provider metering call to assert usage consistency.\n                  reason: Usage metering is provider-owned and outside this boundary.\n                  instead: Assert only on the limiter's own decisions and records.\n              do_not_touch:\n                - Any provider usage metering client, contract, or configuration.\n                - Modules and tests created by the preceding leaves.\n              rollback: Delete the integration test; all lower leaves remain independently proven.\n              observability: A failure identifies which steel-thread step broke by assertion name.\n              execution_recipe: plan-execute-verify\n              proves_capabilities:\n                - POLICY-VALIDATION\n                - POLICY-RESOLUTION-LEG\n                - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT-LEG\n                - DENIAL-CONTRACT\n              sdlc_stages:\n                - test\n\nsteel_thread:\n  - POLICY-DEFINITION\n  - POLICY-RESOLUTION\n  - LIMIT-ENFORCEMENT\n  - DECISION-EVIDENCE\n\nobjections:\n  - id: OBJ-NO-OWNER\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: >-\n      No responsible owner is named for the limiter work; every swimlane carries an\n      unresolved owner. This must be decided before review can bind the topology.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      The intake assigns ownership only for usage metering, to the provider, and places it\n      out of scope. Inventing an owner here would fabricate authority.\n  - id: OBJ-INPROC-STORE\n    status: ACCEPTED\n    summary: >-\n      Enforcement is proven against an in-process store, so the proof covers single-process\n      window semantics only, not concurrent or distributed counting.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Production storage is explicitly not selected in this scope. Distributed counting\n      correctness is separate work that would need its own leaf and renewed authorization.\n  - id: OBJ-REASON-STRING\n    status: OPEN\n    summary: The exact denial reason constant is proposed, not decided.\n    owner: unassigned\n    rationale: >-\n      Callers match on this value, so fixing it is a product decision rather than an\n      implementation detail.\n\ncontentions:\n  - between:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n    resolution: >-\n      Both leaves write src/ratelimit/gate.py. The reason constant must exist before\n      telemetry can carry it, so the writes are serialized in steel-thread order.\n    order:\n      - T-20260914-denial-reason\n      - T-20260914-decision-telemetry\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
